    internal const string Encodings = "{\n  \"$comment\": \"GENERATED by the consumer-owned generator; do not edit. Every label of the Encoding Standard (https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/) with the encoding it names, and every byte of each of the standard's single-byte encodings and of x-user-defined, as every reader of a resource's text must read them. It is the exhaustive half of the rule contract/W3C-SELECTORS.md states of a resource's text; decode-cases.json, beside it and written by hand, holds what is said of a content type, of a label, of a byte-order mark and of malformed UTF-8 and UTF-16. A CASE is an `encoding`, by its name in lower case, and its `labels`, the name among them. A reader given a content type whose charset is any one of the labels, in any case and with ASCII white space around it, does one of three things. `everyByte`: the encoding is a single-byte one, and the reader reads the bytes in it; this is the text the 256 bytes 00 to ff, in that order, are: one character to a byte, and U+FFFD where the encoding gives the byte none. `bytes` and `text`: the reader reads the bytes in the encoding, and these are some bytes in hexadecimal and the text they are, which tell UTF-8, UTF-16BE and UTF-16LE from one another. `refused`: the reader refuses every label of the case, by its name, whatever the bytes are: these are the labels of the standard's `replacement` encoding and of its seven legacy multi-byte encodings (Big5, EUC-JP, EUC-KR, gb18030, GBK, ISO-2022-JP and Shift_JIS), whose bytes each reader's own decoder reads otherwise than the standard and than the others. Every character here that is not printable ASCII is written as an escape. WHERE IT COMES FROM. The labels are read from the npm package @exodus/bytes, an implementation of the standard, at the version the repository's package.json pins (MIT, Exodus Movement); the characters of each byte, and the text of each case's bytes, are what Node's TextDecoder reads; and the generator refuses to write where the two disagree about a label. Every SDK runs it, for every label of every case. Its runs include one through encoding_rs, which is the standard as Firefox has it, and that run is the check of this table against a second implementation, label by label and byte by byte. A reader in another language runs it too.\",\n  \"runBy\": {\n    \"ocaml\": [\n      \"sdk/ocaml/sdk/test/closure_behavior.ml\"\n    ],\n    \"haskell\": [\n      \"sdk/haskell/sdk/test/TextCases.hs\"\n    ],\n    \"typescript\": [\n      \"sdk/typescript/core/src/__tests__/decode-cases.test.ts\"\n    ],\n    \"rust\": [\n      \"sdk/rust/sdk/src/resources.rs\",\n      \"sdk/rust/sdk/tests/decode_cases.rs\"\n    ],\n    \"python\": [\n      \"sdk/python/sdk/tests/test_decode_cases.py\"\n    ],\n    \"go\": [\n      \"sdk/go/resources_test.go\"\n    ],\n    \"kotlin\": [\n      \"sdk/kotlin/sdk/src/test/kotlin/xynolog/resources/DecodeCasesTest.kt\"\n    ],\n    \"swift\": [\n      \"sdk/swift/sdk/Tests/XynologTests/DecodeRepresentationTests.swift\"\n    ],\n    \"csharp\": [\n      \"sdk/csharp/sdk/tests/Xynolog.Tests/RepresentationTests.cs\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"cases\": [\n    {\n      \"encoding\": \"utf-8\",\n      \"labels\": [\n        \"unicode-1-1-utf-8\",\n        \"unicode11utf8\",\n        \"unicode20utf8\",\n        \"utf-8\",\n        \"utf8\",\n        \"x-unicode20utf8\"\n      ],\n      \"bytes\": \"c3 a9\",\n      \"text\": \"\\u00e9\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"encoding\": \"utf-16be\",\n      \"labels\": [\n        \"unicodefffe\",\n        \"utf-16be\"\n      ],\n      \"bytes\": \"00 e9\",\n      \"text\": \"\\u00e9\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"encoding\": \"utf-16le\",\n      \"labels\": [\n        \"csunicode\",\n        \"iso-10646-ucs-2\",\n        \"ucs-2\",\n        \"unicode\",\n        \"unicodefeff\",\n        \"utf-16\",\n        \"utf-16le\"\n      ],\n      \"bytes\": \"e9 00\",\n      \"text\": \"\\u00e9\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"encoding\": \"ibm866\",\n      \"labels